course: dictionary
lesson: 3984
title: Gale: wind, laughter, or a name?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A gale is a very strong wind. It is stronger than a breeze, but weaker than a hurricane. Sailors and forecasters pay close attention to gales because they can be dangerous.
explain: Look at the context. «NASA's Curiosity rover has been exploring Gale Crater since 2012.» Gale starts with a capital letter and is the name of a crater, which is a large hole in the ground, so it is not a wind here. Capital letters and the words around a word are both clues.
explain: Gale can also appear in a phrase. «Gales of laughter» means a sudden, loud burst of laughing. The word laughter tells you the meaning fits a laugh, not the weather.
explain: To check your meaning, put it into the sentence and see if it still makes sense. Try «The wind blew a gale» and «laughter blew a gale» to see which one works.
fact: A gale is a very strong wind, stronger than a breeze but weaker than a hurricane.
fact: On the Beaufort scale, force 8 is a gale.
fact: Weather forecasts give «gale warnings» to sailors.
fact: In a gale, stay away from trees, the sea and loose objects.
fact: «Gales of laughter» means lots of loud laughing, and «Gale Crater» is a crater on Mars, a name with a capital letter.
exercise: «NASA's Curiosity rover has been exploring Gale Crater since 2012.» Does gale mean a strong wind here? | Yes, a gentle breeze | Yes, a very strong wind | Yes, a sudden loud laugh | *No, it is the name of a crater
exercise: Forecasters issue «gale warnings» for the sea. What do these warnings tell sailors? | Trees will grow faster | Fish are biting well | *Very strong winds are coming | A sunny day is coming
exercise: «The whole class burst into gales of laughter.» What does gales of laughter mean? | Strong winds blowing across the field | Sad crying | *Lots of loud laughing | Quiet smiling
test: What is a gale? | A hot, sunny day | A gentle rain | A light breeze | *A very strong wind
test: On the Beaufort scale, which force is a gale? | Force 1 | *Force 8 | Force 3 | Force 5
exam: Which sentence uses gale to mean a burst of laughing? | «Gale Crater is on Mars.» | «The sailors read the gale warning.» | «The gale ripped the roof off the barn.» | *«Gales of laughter filled the classroom.»
